---

<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Combine ages, questionnaire answers, visit periods and causes in one private table,
then use it to compare causes across age groups.
</div>

**You need:** the Opal login from [Getting started](book-00-start.html), plus
the date and category lessons. This chapter uses `omop.questionnaire`.

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.questionnaire", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("questionnaire")$conns
```

## Find the concepts

```{r va-discovery}
catalogue <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(c(900001L, 900002L, 900101L, 900102L,
  900201L, 900202L), conns = conns)$pooled
catalogue[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "domain_id")]
```

The questions are Education **900001** and Fever **900002**; answers Secondary
**900101** and University **900102** are distinct from the questions. The coded
causes are Myocardial infarction **900201** and Pneumonia **900202**.
Their occurrence was established in [chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html).

## Define one row per person

Keep people without visits, answers or deaths. Read the latest nonmissing answer
from `value_as_concept_id`, and the cause from `cause_concept_id`. These definitions
use all recorded history; the exercise below restricts education to before death.

```{r answers}
education_event <- omop_event("observation", 900001L)
education <- omop_variable(event = education_event,
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
fever <- omop_variable(event = omop_event("observation", 900002L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
cause <- omop_variable(event = omop_event("death"),
  reducer = omop_reduce("first_value", value = "cause_concept_id"))
```

Completed age uses the full birth date and the chosen anchor. An incomplete birth
date or absent anchor gives missing age; it does not silently create a birthday.
All calendar presentations below refer to the same first visit.

```{r ages-and-dates}
age_at_death <- omop_age_at("death")
first_visit <- omop_anchor(omop_event("visit_occurrence"), which = "first")
answer_keys <- c("concept:900101", "concept:900102")
variables <- list(
  age_at_death = age_at_death,
  age_band = omop_number_bins(age_at_death, breaks = c(0, 40, 121),
    labels = c("under 40", "40 and over")),
  age_at_first_visit = omop_age_at(first_visit),
  education = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(levels = answer_keys,
    order = omop_category_order(c(answer_keys, "special:missing")), ordered = TRUE,
    missing = omop_category_missing(na = "level"))),
  fever = fever, cause = cause,
  visit_date = omop_date(first_visit),
  visit_month = omop_date(first_visit, omop_date_spec(precision = "month")),
  visit_quarter = omop_date(first_visit, omop_date_spec(precision = "quarter")),
  visit_year = omop_date(first_visit, omop_date_spec(precision = "year", representation = "number")))
capstone <- omop_recipe(variables = variables,
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
result <- recipe_execute(capstone, out = c(people = "D_capstone"), conns = conns)
```

The table stays on the server with a pseudonymous person key. Exact visit dates
remain private; we use month and quarter categories for calendar counts.

## Check age and missingness

```{r age-counts}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_capstone$age_at_death",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Ages 32, 33, 42 and 43 each occur six times. The 24 missing ages combine 12 deaths
with incomplete birth dates and 12 survivors. Missing age is not a fifth age band.

```{r first-visit-age}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_capstone$age_at_first_visit", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.numNA("D_capstone$age_at_first_visit", datasources = conns)
```

There are 24 valid ages and 24 missing. The valid ages are 29 and 39, with a mean
of 34 years. These describe the age at first care, not the later age at death.

## Read the answers and calendar periods

```{r answer-counts}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_capstone$education",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_capstone$fever",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

Education has 12 Secondary, 12 University and 24 Missing answers. Fever has 12
Yes, 12 No and 24 missing answers. No is an observed response; it is not a substitute for missingness.

```{r visit-periods}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_capstone$visit_month",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_capstone$visit_quarter",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

March, June and July each contain 12 first visits, with 12 people lacking a visit.
They fall in three different quarters, so changing precision leaves these counts
unchanged. In other data, several months may merge into one quarter.

## Use the table: cause by age at death

```{r cause-age}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_capstone$cause",
  "D_capstone$age_band", useNA = "always",
  datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

The 12 pneumonia deaths are under 40. Of 24 myocardial-infarction deaths, 12 are
40 or over and 12 have missing age. The 12 survivors have neither cause nor age
at death. These are counts, not age-specific mortality rates: rates require age
and person-time for survivors too, which the next chapter supplies.
Fine cross-tabulations may be withheld when cells are too small; a withheld cell
is not zero.

## Exercises

**1. Use only education recorded by death.** Keep the same people and compare
the education counts. Explain what happens to survivors.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r education-before-death}
before_death <- omop_variable(event = education_event,
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id",
    window = omop_window(NULL, 0, anchor = omop_anchor(omop_event("death"), which = "first"))))
restricted <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = before_death))
recipe_execute(restricted, out = "D_before_death", conns = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_before_death$education",
  useNA = "always", datasources = conns)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
```

The counts remain 12 Secondary, 12 University and 24 missing: all observed
answers preceded death. This exercise uses the raw education variable without
the category specification, so missing answers have the raw `NA` label instead
of the main table's “Missing”. A survivor has no death anchor, so this definition cannot
supply an answer for that person. The unchanged totals do not make the window irrelevant.

</details>

**2. Use year as a number.** Find the mean first-visit year and its denominator.
Then check how its type differs from the exact date and month category.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r year-and-types, table.row.names = FALSE}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_capstone$visit_year", datasources = conns)
columns <- recipe_codebook(result)[[1]]$columns
columns[columns$name %in% c("visit_date", "visit_month", "visit_year"), c("name", "r_type")]
```

All 36 visitors have year 2020, so its mean is 2020; 12 people have no value.
The codebook distinguishes Date, factor and numeric representations. Averaging
calendar years is possible, but it cannot reveal the distribution across months.

</details>

## Recap

One person-level table can combine answers, completed ages and calendar summaries.
Its usefulness comes from a question answered with those columns, while retaining
missingness and keeping patient rows private.

**Next:** [19. Case study: cause-specific mortality by age, sex and period](book-19-case-mortality.html).
